        <div><a name="_english" id="_english"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.11 English</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2e206a05-281e-4f70-af89-c1cf3df7bdeb" id="_2e206a05-281e-4f70-af89-c1cf3df7bdeb"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    given name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    family name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_afe897e4-ef2a-4dca-8b35-c84fded89dc4" id="_afe897e4-ef2a-4dca-8b35-c84fded89dc4"></a>English names are names used in, or originating in, England. In England as
elsewhere in the English-speaking world, a complete name usually consists of a
given name, commonly referred to as a first name or Christian name, and a (most
commonly patrilineal) family name or surname, also referred to as a last name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_da7c0321-2a5f-472d-a207-ccada599a2fa" id="_da7c0321-2a5f-472d-a207-ccada599a2fa"></a>There can be several given names, some of these being often referred to as a
second name, or middle name(s).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_05146b77-2797-4ecc-ac8e-246642cc2f01" id="_05146b77-2797-4ecc-ac8e-246642cc2f01"></a>Double-barrelled names, compound surnames in English feature two words,
are often joined by a hyphen or hyphens.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns" id="_typical_name_patterns"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.11.1 Typical name patterns</p>

  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.24</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_a110b06e-f41c-44f6-8d80-97ccfe1e8504" id="_a110b06e-f41c-44f6-8d80-97ccfe1e8504"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples" id="_examples"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.11.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_139befb9-240c-4ba3-a305-5dd78d90db7d" id="_139befb9-240c-4ba3-a305-5dd78d90db7d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Henry Hepburne-Scott</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_c9a94f8c-c3c5-4937-8696-eb3bce92e0a3" id="_c9a94f8c-c3c5-4937-8696-eb3bce92e0a3"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Some families having as many as
three or four words making up their surname.</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_25ac692b-d5ad-4f12-8579-5e7cf30dbf87" id="_25ac692b-d5ad-4f12-8579-5e7cf30dbf87"></a>Charles Hepburn-Stuart-Forbes-Trefusis, 21st Baron Clinton.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_2c26f276-4fd8-4b9a-b9fb-2d94f156bbbe" id="_2c26f276-4fd8-4b9a-b9fb-2d94f156bbbe"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>It is not unusual for compound surnames to be composed of separate words
not linked by a hyphen.</p><p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.25</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_3e073ed2-2b64-4c6f-917a-81fce612373b" id="_3e073ed2-2b64-4c6f-917a-81fce612373b"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Iain</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Duncan Smith</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_french" id="_french"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.12 French</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_984b3470-8c6c-4fa0-98fa-2c9c2f73ed6f" id="_984b3470-8c6c-4fa0-98fa-2c9c2f73ed6f"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo21;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo21;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7f4a6f1a-5c1f-4e40-960d-dab871df386d" id="_7f4a6f1a-5c1f-4e40-960d-dab871df386d"></a>French people have one, two or more given names (first names). One of them
is used in daily life.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_2" id="_typical_name_patterns_2"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.12.1 Typical name patterns</p><p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.26</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_2e30e8cf-b256-4ce1-aa81-e47876a5f702" id="_2e30e8cf-b256-4ce1-aa81-e47876a5f702"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.27</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_f2a6cf9e-5b8f-409a-abca-051c0d7d105e" id="_f2a6cf9e-5b8f-409a-abca-051c0d7d105e"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
<div><a name="_examples_2" id="_examples_2"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.12.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_b867e150-5575-4404-ab5c-62c108519377" id="_b867e150-5575-4404-ab5c-62c108519377"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Jacques Chirac&#x201D; instead of officially of Jacques Ren&#xE9; Chirac.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_d4877906-6f41-469d-a1d9-fc4d2c391155" id="_d4877906-6f41-469d-a1d9-fc4d2c391155"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Philippe P&#xE9;tain&#x201D; instead of officially of Henri Philippe P&#xE9;tain,
because Philippe was the given name that he used in daily
life.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b51a3af0-11db-4412-9cbb-b843636846b5" id="_b51a3af0-11db-4412-9cbb-b843636846b5"></a>Middle names in the English sense do not exist, and middle initials are
never used for second or further given names.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_a9961394-250b-47d3-9318-3fa3225e28c8" id="_a9961394-250b-47d3-9318-3fa3225e28c8"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Claude All&#xE8;gre may be cited as &#x201C;Claude J. All&#xE8;gre&#x201D; in English-speaking
scientific publications, but not in French ones.</p></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_german" id="_german"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.13 German</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_777c274d-46b8-48fb-bf12-3af2c9982f0e" id="_777c274d-46b8-48fb-bf12-3af2c9982f0e"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo22;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo22;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_fad225ee-d4b1-4c88-831e-26aec0fe44fb" id="_fad225ee-d4b1-4c88-831e-26aec0fe44fb"></a>Personal names in German-speaking Europe consist of one or several given names
(Vorname, plural Vornamen) and a surname (Nachname, Familienname). The Vorname
is usually gender-specific.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_3" id="_typical_name_patterns_3"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.13.1 Typical name patterns</p>

  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.28</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_458b4e66-e7a8-4c45-9c01-ef8558bce903" id="_458b4e66-e7a8-4c45-9c01-ef8558bce903"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples_3" id="_examples_3"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.13.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_4427650e-b36a-44f0-846f-0361d2754d9a" id="_4427650e-b36a-44f0-846f-0361d2754d9a"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Otto Eduard Leopold F&#xFC;rst von Bismarck.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_bf9ffeba-7fa6-4a57-8f38-fcd099853c8a" id="_bf9ffeba-7fa6-4a57-8f38-fcd099853c8a"></a>The Vorname (in English forename) is usually given to a child by the parents
shortly after birth. It is common to give a child several Vornamen (forenames).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7e8da3c9-3802-4167-afd3-788ee10e135e" id="_7e8da3c9-3802-4167-afd3-788ee10e135e"></a>The Rufname (&#x201C;appellation name&#x201D; or &#x201C;call name&#x201D;) is the given name
intended for everyday use, and is often underlined on official documents.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_2288c251-8193-47ed-904a-91676178d0f0" id="_2288c251-8193-47ed-904a-91676178d0f0"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The Rufname of the mathematician Amalie Emmy Noether is &#x201C;Emmy&#x201D;.</p></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_greek" id="_greek"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.14 Greek</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_hindu" id="_hindu"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.15 Hindu</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_hungarian" id="_hungarian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.16 Hungarian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d569b243-0797-4532-92c4-c48e3f4915b9" id="_d569b243-0797-4532-92c4-c48e3f4915b9"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo23;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    given name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo23;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    family name
  </p>

<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_4" id="_typical_name_patterns_4"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.16.1 Typical name patterns</p>

  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.29</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_da833a82-4752-4508-aab9-80ea75bf05e3" id="_da833a82-4752-4508-aab9-80ea75bf05e3"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples_4" id="_examples_4"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.16.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_85e03188-36f9-4f6e-9dcb-bb17591fcf9b" id="_85e03188-36f9-4f6e-9dcb-bb17591fcf9b"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Kova&#x301;cs Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_13a62687-4b26-493b-b267-5af67b11c3f7" id="_13a62687-4b26-493b-b267-5af67b11c3f7"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>If there is more than one given name, they come in order of importance
after the family name:</p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo24;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Kova&#x301;cs Ja&#x301;nos Alada&#x301;r
  </p>
</div>
<div class="example"><a name="_2f9f3e03-461b-493c-a8d5-13896a02e309" id="_2f9f3e03-461b-493c-a8d5-13896a02e309"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>There can be more than one family name, which can be written fully or abbreviated
into initials:</p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo25;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Segesdy Kova&#x301;cs Ja&#x301;nos
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo25;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Segesdy-Kova&#x301;cs Ja&#x301;nos
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo25;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    S.Kova&#x301;cs Ja&#x301;nos
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo25;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    Segesdy K. Ja&#x301;nos
  </p>
</div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_icelandic" id="_icelandic"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.17 Icelandic</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c851ae2f-7074-4a0f-a4f9-8280af51ea92" id="_c851ae2f-7074-4a0f-a4f9-8280af51ea92"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo26;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    given name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo26;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    patronymic name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo26;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    matronymic name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo26;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    genonymic name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo26;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    family name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7e0a6976-e88c-43a0-a680-5ff2b3241cae" id="_7e0a6976-e88c-43a0-a680-5ff2b3241cae"></a>Icelandic names differ from most current Western family name systems by being
patronymic or occasionally matronymic: they indicate the father (or mother) of
the child and not the historic family lineage.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_0d6911a8-56b5-492c-b759-f7b58d0662f6" id="_0d6911a8-56b5-492c-b759-f7b58d0662f6"></a>Iceland&#x2019;s traditional name system was formerly used by all Nordic countries
except partly Finland.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_97b19c56-1353-4011-9741-d51331e6ad9b" id="_97b19c56-1353-4011-9741-d51331e6ad9b"></a>The Icelandic system is not based on family names
(although some people do have family names and might use both systems).
Generally, a person&#x2019;s second name indicates the first name of their father
(patronymic) or in some cases mother (matronymic). According to Icelandic
naming tradition, second names end in -son or -d&#xF3;ttir with few exceptions.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_5" id="_typical_name_patterns_5"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.17.1 Typical name patterns</p><p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.30</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_58fe93cb-7115-4dc3-9dc7-f2084822dd2a" id="_58fe93cb-7115-4dc3-9dc7-f2084822dd2a"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">patronymic or matronymic name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.31</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_3bb09033-d05f-48d8-8e76-1aca66902747" id="_3bb09033-d05f-48d8-8e76-1aca66902747"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">patronymic or matronymic name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">genonymic name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.32</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_c4a04cd0-138d-4a38-8e21-920983590434" id="_c4a04cd0-138d-4a38-8e21-920983590434"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">patronymic or matronymic name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">(genonymic name)</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
<div><a name="_examples_5" id="_examples_5"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.17.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_5e46ab88-3a2d-4715-b5bb-0749078fd4c9" id="_5e46ab88-3a2d-4715-b5bb-0749078fd4c9"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>J&#xF3;hanna Sigur&#xF0;ard&#xF3;ttir. &#x201C;J&#xF3;hanna, daughter of Sigur&#xF0;ur&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_780942a2-86ef-4fde-807d-c7d9b82e2fb5" id="_780942a2-86ef-4fde-807d-c7d9b82e2fb5"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Matronymic. Hei&#xF0;ar Helguson, &#x201C;Helguson, son of Helga&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_fa3de962-5310-4fb4-b247-351c65d8240a" id="_fa3de962-5310-4fb4-b247-351c65d8240a"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#xD6;rvar &#xDE;&#xF3;reyjarson Sm&#xE1;rason. (&#xD6;rvar, son of &#xDE;&#xF3;rey, son of Sm&#xE1;ri)</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_4b966794-aa2a-4c99-a97b-70439f8b7b57" id="_4b966794-aa2a-4c99-a97b-70439f8b7b57"></a>Some family names do exist in Iceland, most commonly adaptations from last name
patronyms Icelanders took up when living abroad.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_0be4311f-f8e1-4058-b801-5d49e77cd404" id="_0be4311f-f8e1-4058-b801-5d49e77cd404"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Geir Haarde, Ei&#xF0;ur Sm&#xE1;ri Gu&#xF0;johnsen, Anita Briem.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_221a8d18-d088-46b1-a25d-43aadc1fb5d5" id="_221a8d18-d088-46b1-a25d-43aadc1fb5d5"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  5</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Nobel Prize-winning author Halld&#xF3;r Laxness, born as Halld&#xF3;r Gu&#xF0;j&#xF3;nsson.</p></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_indian" id="_indian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.18 Indian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_indonesian" id="_indonesian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.19 Indonesian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c786dbc7-c2b8-44c2-acfe-04ad0607c6cc" id="_c786dbc7-c2b8-44c2-acfe-04ad0607c6cc"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo27;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    given name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo27;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo27;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    patronymic name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_1053082d-3b46-40aa-9f56-6d116e97ade1" id="_1053082d-3b46-40aa-9f56-6d116e97ade1"></a>Indonesians generally use given names without a family name.
The majority of Indonesians do not have family names. Rather,
their given names are geographically and culturally specific.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_3dcccf47-bab2-4b2a-9460-bff0e2f5d45e" id="_3dcccf47-bab2-4b2a-9460-bff0e2f5d45e"></a>Some given names include a family name or a patronymic.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_6" id="_typical_name_patterns_6"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.19.1 Typical name patterns</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_45e3303e-d3a9-4e20-9ab8-aaf670d3b7d3" id="_45e3303e-d3a9-4e20-9ab8-aaf670d3b7d3"></a>Indonesian names usually fall into one of the following categories:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo28;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Mononymic names
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo28;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Polynymic names without a family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo28;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Polynymic names with a family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo28;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    Polynymic names with patronymic
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c78a936c-6107-4f33-bd06-08bafcce5870" id="_c78a936c-6107-4f33-bd06-08bafcce5870"></a>An Indonesian name is often mis-represented in other cultures, such as:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo29;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Netherlands: the word &#x201C;Onbekend&#x201D; (&#x201C;unknown&#x201D;) is assigned as a last name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo29;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Germany: the one-word name is used as both given name and surname
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo29;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    US: &#x201C;Fnu&#x201D; (family name unknown) is assigned as the first or given name, &#x201C;Lnu&#x201D; (last name unknown) is assigned as the family, surname or last name,
or the name is duplicated as the surname.
  </p>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples_6" id="_examples_6"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.19.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_414a32a5-af0b-429a-81f7-607081a958bd" id="_414a32a5-af0b-429a-81f7-607081a958bd"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Mulyono&#x201D;, the original name of &#x201C;Joko Widodo.&#x201D;</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_4ae91dd3-2f5f-44a3-93c0-5cdc3d622638" id="_4ae91dd3-2f5f-44a3-93c0-5cdc3d622638"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Kusno Sosrodihardjo</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_3194c4b1-4a5c-457e-b043-5321d58ecac7" id="_3194c4b1-4a5c-457e-b043-5321d58ecac7"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Fransiskus Xaverius Hadi Rudyatmo</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_ec9eca13-8ad3-4e26-a75d-17a00777a7aa" id="_ec9eca13-8ad3-4e26-a75d-17a00777a7aa"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Sudono Salim</p></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_iranian" id="_iranian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.20 Iranian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_iraqi" id="_iraqi"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.21 Iraqi</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_italian" id="_italian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.22 Italian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_47964d01-fea5-4ef9-9d9d-ca940de690fa" id="_47964d01-fea5-4ef9-9d9d-ca940de690fa"></a>A name in Italian consists of a given name (nome) and a surname (cognome).
Surnames are normally written after given names.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_72d1825a-b8ae-4b96-bf73-7155bd657408" id="_72d1825a-b8ae-4b96-bf73-7155bd657408"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo30;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo30;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>
</div>
        <div><a name="_japanese" id="_japanese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.23 Japanese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_eeb384ab-a332-41a5-9778-cc124747ff0d" id="_eeb384ab-a332-41a5-9778-cc124747ff0d"></a>Japanese names usually consist of a family name (surname),
followed by a given name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_91cea7f6-daf3-48ba-b6c1-b54fbb3aadd6" id="_91cea7f6-daf3-48ba-b6c1-b54fbb3aadd6"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo31;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo31;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_1c951d00-c1c7-4cda-91bf-3e125f7579a8" id="_1c951d00-c1c7-4cda-91bf-3e125f7579a8"></a>Japanese names are usually written in kanji,
where characters are logographic, but each with a variety of
possible Japanese pronunciations. Therefore
often names cannot in general be spelled or
pronounced unless both the spelling and pronunciation are given.</p>
<span style="MsoCommentReference" target="2" class="commentLink" from="" to=""><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:9.0pt" xml:lang="EN-GB"><a style="mso-comment-reference:SMC_2;mso-comment-date:20190808T000000Z"></a><span style="mso-special-character:comment" target="2"></span></span></span>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_7" id="_typical_name_patterns_7"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.23.1 Typical name patterns</p>

  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.33</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_4cbaca98-fbe6-4136-9ff0-fb3cd9acba20" id="_4cbaca98-fbe6-4136-9ff0-fb3cd9acba20"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples_7" id="_examples_7"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.23.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_615ef19e-7d39-41e7-9d5e-4cd9a03196d1" id="_615ef19e-7d39-41e7-9d5e-4cd9a03196d1"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x7FD4; is traditionally pronounced &#x201C;Hiroto&#x201D;, but can also be pronounced as
&#x201C;Haruto&#x201D;, &#x201C;Yamato&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taiga&#x201D;, &#x201C;Sora&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taito&#x201D;, &#x201C;Daito&#x201D;, and
&#x201C;Masato&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_d320aa6d-144f-42da-bc37-6c364620013c" id="_d320aa6d-144f-42da-bc37-6c364620013c"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x548C; can be read as Daiwa or Yamato.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_03a43100-8fda-4515-b99a-0469ff12a43c" id="_03a43100-8fda-4515-b99a-0469ff12a43c"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x6771;&#x6D77;&#x6797; can be read as T&#x14D;kairin or Sh&#x14D;ji</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_8c5b8de6-07c1-42f6-8faa-bbbd441760b9" id="_8c5b8de6-07c1-42f6-8faa-bbbd441760b9"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;&#x4E2D;&#x5C71;&#x6A35;&#x201D; (Nakayama Sho), the Japanese pseudonym of
&#x201C;Sun Yat-sen&#x201D;:</p><p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.34</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_819cf44e-aab3-4a7b-81a4-6f1738edc0e2" id="_819cf44e-aab3-4a7b-81a4-6f1738edc0e2"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">&#x4E2D;&#x5C71;</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">&#x6A35;</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div>
<div><a name="_japanese_immigrants_in_brazil" id="_japanese_immigrants_in_brazil"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.23.3 Japanese immigrants in Brazil</p><div class="example"><a name="_e8372bf8-139f-49b2-bd22-85ddd7af9b0c" id="_e8372bf8-139f-49b2-bd22-85ddd7af9b0c"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span></p>
  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.35</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_d659ffa4-0d0c-4fd3-88df-b84851bf362e" id="_d659ffa4-0d0c-4fd3-88df-b84851bf362e"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Akemi</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Denise</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Sameshima</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div class="example"><a name="_d2309a6c-475f-4606-a4b8-b7fdb42f536d" id="_d2309a6c-475f-4606-a4b8-b7fdb42f536d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span></p>
  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.36</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_7097e68f-3855-49e0-83b2-39db21106cb6" id="_7097e68f-3855-49e0-83b2-39db21106cb6"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Yuri</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Vanessa</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Ohara</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_jewish" id="_jewish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.24 Jewish</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_55060c25-7aa8-4b33-b3ad-5c0efd6613a4" id="_55060c25-7aa8-4b33-b3ad-5c0efd6613a4"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo32;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo32;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    given name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo32;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    patronymic name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_0d08c2c1-b80f-4617-a49e-245ca61fe684" id="_0d08c2c1-b80f-4617-a49e-245ca61fe684"></a>Modern Jewish names include Hebrew patronymic names as well as family names.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_8" id="_typical_name_patterns_8"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.24.1 Typical name patterns</p><p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.37</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_dd81d4e9-7142-474d-baf8-028c40e61330" id="_dd81d4e9-7142-474d-baf8-028c40e61330"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">patronymic name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.38</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_35a7715d-4acf-48cb-bf86-306513ece399" id="_35a7715d-4acf-48cb-bf86-306513ece399"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
<div><a name="_examples_8" id="_examples_8"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.24.2 Examples</p>

</div></div>
        <div><a name="_korean" id="_korean"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.25 Korean</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_4048ee9d-527a-4901-9cb6-8be27eeebb4c" id="_4048ee9d-527a-4901-9cb6-8be27eeebb4c"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo33;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo33;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_613194bc-7090-46c2-a749-969a9e9aaef6" id="_613194bc-7090-46c2-a749-969a9e9aaef6"></a>A Korean name consists of a family name followed by a given name, as used by
the Korean people in both South Korea and North Korea.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_45fea8d0-6a70-451a-89e5-9cdbdd28885a" id="_45fea8d0-6a70-451a-89e5-9cdbdd28885a"></a>In the Korean language,
ireum or seongmyeong usually refers to the family name (seong) and given name
(myeong) together.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_9" id="_typical_name_patterns_9"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.25.1 Typical name patterns</p>

  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.39</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_81205ea1-aec0-4701-bf44-c3152a285fec" id="_81205ea1-aec0-4701-bf44-c3152a285fec"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples_9" id="_examples_9"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.25.2 Examples</p>

</div></div>
        <div><a name="_kurdish" id="_kurdish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.26 Kurdish</p>

</div>
